The variable `s` is declared as type `Sensor`, but you're trying to call `readValue()` on it. Since `readValue()` is not part of the `Sensor` interface, this won't compile.

What's the main issue here?

The class doesn't properly implement the interface because it's missing implementations for the required methods `read()` and `info()`. Also, the method called `readValue()` in the implementing class doesn't match any method signature from the interface.

Can you identify what needs to be fixed to make this compile correctly?

```java
interface Sensor {
    int LIMIT = 100;
    void read();
    static void info() { System.out.println("Sensor limit = " + LIMIT); }
}
class TempSensor implements Sensor {
    @Override public void read() { System.out.println("Temperature read"); }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Sensor sensor = new TempSensor();
        Sensor.info();
        sensor.read();
    }
}
```
